In article <1990May8.065725.17192@tolerant.com> craig@tolsoft.UUCP (Craig Harmer) writes:
>4) SIGSEGV and SIGBUS dumps don't cause a game to be saved, while
>	SIGHUP does.  why not?  is this some sort of weird anti-cheating
>	thing (preventing you from taking advantage of bugs that cause
>	SIGSEGV or SIGBUS)?  it seems to cause more harm than good.
>	

[patch deleted]

To Craig:

I have been complaining about this sort of thing for ages.  Leonid
Levin suggested fixing it recently and a few ignorant hackers flamed
him.  I hope that your fix works.  Explanations and suggestions follow.

SEGV and BUS signals are usually errors, whereas HUP is not.  That is
why nethack saves in the latter case.  When you restore after a SEGV
or BUS error, does it usually work?  I suggest including a warning, like
"Restoring saved game ...--More--
 Game saved after bus error.  Proceed at your own risk."

Sometimes nethack saves in a .e file after it catches its own error.
These files always seem playable after a restore.  Why not get rid of
the .e, and just include the warning.  This would reduce embarassing
email to sysadmins.

-- Richard

